WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.751
A collection may contain hundreds of

00:00:02.771 --> 00:00:04.348
millions of records.

00:00:04.511 --> 00:00:07.261
Must a Boolean search reread all of them

00:00:07.281 --> 00:00:08.575
whenever you type a query?

00:00:08.725 --> 00:00:11.719
Imagine doing that for every new search.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.534
An inverted index provides a different

00:00:15.554 --> 00:00:18.318
route. Start from an analysed term and

00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:20.624
follow its pointers to records containing

00:00:20.644 --> 00:00:23.498
it. Our four-record illustration shows the

00:00:23.518 --> 00:00:26.423
mechanism; it is not a speed benchmark.

00:00:27.341 --> 00:00:30.226
The chapter starts with D one: Delulu job

00:00:30.246 --> 00:00:31.798
interview expectations.

00:00:31.973 --> 00:00:34.607
D two: Job interview preparation guide.

00:00:34.769 --> 00:00:38.744
D three: Unrealistic job expectations.

00:00:38.919 --> 00:00:41.411
D four: Delulu about my dream job.

00:00:41.984 --> 00:00:44.838
Document first means opening a record and

00:00:44.858 --> 00:00:46.139
reading its words.

00:00:46.301 --> 00:00:49.116
Term first reverses the direction: begin

00:00:49.136 --> 00:00:51.410
with a dictionary term, then look up its

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.052
posting list. A posting identifies a

00:00:54.072 --> 00:00:56.435
document containing that analysed term.

00:00:57.355 --> 00:00:59.620
In the selected dictionary, delulu points

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.849
to D one and D four.

00:01:02.024 --> 00:01:04.504
Interview points to D one and D two.

00:01:04.666 --> 00:01:06.282
Job points to all four.

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:08.950
These are selected lists, not every term

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:09.916
in the collection.

00:01:10.495 --> 00:01:13.310
For delulu AND interview, intersect the

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:16.466
two lists. D one is the shared identifier,

00:01:16.603 --> 00:01:18.348
so D one passes.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.990
The engine does not need to reread all

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.605
four texts to make this Boolean decision.

00:01:24.514 --> 00:01:26.798
Use OR instead, and combine the

00:01:26.818 --> 00:01:30.573
identifiers: D one, D two and D four.

00:01:30.749 --> 00:01:33.525
Use delulu NOT interview, and remove the

00:01:33.545 --> 00:01:36.553
overlap: only D four remains.

00:01:37.098 --> 00:01:39.040
The same posting lists support different

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.186
admission rules.

00:01:40.361 --> 00:01:42.210
The dictionary tells you which lists to

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.723
consult. The Boolean operator tells you

00:01:44.743 --> 00:01:45.766
how to combine them.

00:01:45.928 --> 00:01:49.130
Neither step, by itself, decides the best

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.075
order for the resulting records.

00:01:51.987 --> 00:01:54.336
Two properties make this efficient.

00:01:54.499 --> 00:01:57.198
Selective access goes directly to the

00:01:57.218 --> 00:02:00.020
query terms' lists instead of scanning

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.114
every document.

00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:03.550
Rare terms tend to have shorter lists.

00:02:03.712 --> 00:02:06.553
Ordered access keeps identifiers sorted,

00:02:06.676 --> 00:02:08.911
so intersection can move forwards through

00:02:08.931 --> 00:02:09.864
the lists.

00:02:10.413 --> 00:02:12.975
Our moving pointers compare identifiers,

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:16.004
advance the smaller one, and retain an

00:02:16.024 --> 00:02:17.279
equal identifier.

00:02:17.441 --> 00:02:20.874
Appendix C develops the fuller mechanism.

00:02:21.037 --> 00:02:23.697
This is an algorithm illustration, not a

00:02:23.717 --> 00:02:26.158
universal response-time promise.

00:02:27.055 --> 00:02:29.439
Real postings may also record term

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:32.777
frequency, the field containing a term and

00:02:32.797 --> 00:02:35.199
its positions. These additions support

00:02:35.219 --> 00:02:37.738
scoring, field weighting, phrase

00:02:37.758 --> 00:02:40.598
searching, proximity and highlighting.

00:02:40.748 --> 00:02:43.202
A list of identifiers is the simplest

00:02:43.222 --> 00:02:44.245
teaching view.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.833
The analysis that created the dictionary

00:02:46.853 --> 00:02:49.372
also affects these entries and the

00:02:49.392 --> 00:02:51.485
collection statistics used by later

00:02:51.505 --> 00:02:53.186
lexical ranking methods.

00:02:53.361 --> 00:02:55.763
Text analysis has shaped the evidence

00:02:55.783 --> 00:02:58.714
before a ranking formula ever runs.

00:02:59.619 --> 00:03:02.219
In search engineering, full-text search

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:04.435
names a capability for searching

00:03:04.455 --> 00:03:06.290
natural-language text fields.

00:03:06.466 --> 00:03:08.275
Those fields might contain titles and

00:03:08.295 --> 00:03:12.257
abstracts, selected passages or OCR text.

00:03:12.432 --> 00:03:13.868
They need not contain a complete

00:03:13.888 --> 00:03:14.937
publication.

00:03:15.514 --> 00:03:18.167
Conversely, possessing a complete article

00:03:18.187 --> 00:03:21.156
does not guarantee every part is indexed.

00:03:21.331 --> 00:03:23.940
The label also specifies no single

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.336
admission rule or ranking method.

00:03:26.486 --> 00:03:29.004
Ask which text and units are searchable,

00:03:29.179 --> 00:03:31.633
how analysis treats them, and how matches

00:03:31.653 --> 00:03:33.024
are admitted and ranked.

00:03:33.936 --> 00:03:36.794
We can now follow the whole chain: text,

00:03:36.814 --> 00:03:40.016
tokens, analysed terms, a dictionary with

00:03:40.036 --> 00:03:43.031
posting lists, and Boolean set operations.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.480
Compatible analysis matters on both

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.567
document and query sides.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.328
The result is an eligible set.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.867
To order it by estimated relevance, the

00:03:52.887 --> 00:03:55.469
system still needs a scoring rule.

00:03:55.632 --> 00:03:57.983
That is Chapter Three's question: how does

00:03:58.003 --> 00:04:00.534
a lexical system rank the records that got

00:04:00.554 --> 00:04:01.256
through?
